WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.900
Speaker 1: This is a really interesting sign.&nbsp;
A whole bunch of Republicans who won their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:15.480
2022 primaries wiped mentions of failed former&nbsp;
President Donald Trump from their websites after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:22.320
their primary as they reset their campaigns for&nbsp;
the general election in November. This signals an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:27.780
arguably significant change within the Republican&nbsp;
electorate, at least as far as the candidates are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:31.740
perceiving. So let's talk about it. There's a&nbsp;
good Daily Beast article by Sam Brody, who's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:37.920
congressional reporter, which points out and is&nbsp;
titled Republican Candidates Strip Abortion and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.120
Trump Connections from their Campaign Websites.&nbsp;
These Republicans have made a calculation. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:47.640
made the calculation during the primary among&nbsp;
the Republican electorate. It's good to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.500
up our connection to Trump in our support of&nbsp;
Donald Trump. Once they won their primary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:57.840
their view changed and they said, okay, for the&nbsp;
general election electorate, I am now better off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.860
removing mentions of Donald Trump. And I think&nbsp;
when we look at exactly who these candidates are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:05.100
it will start to make a lot of sense. And we're&nbsp;
going to link to this article and you can check&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:11.040
it out. And I'm getting a paywall and let me&nbsp;
just see if I can deal with that so that we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:17.760
actually look at the article that popped up all&nbsp;
of a sudden. And because of clarity, I really want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:24.780
to. Here we go. Okay. Right after Beau Hynes won&nbsp;
a crowded primary from Congress in North Carolina,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:31.920
a visitor to the campaign website would&nbsp;
immediately find his declaration that he was 100%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:40.380
pro-life and 100% pro-Trump. Pretty clear. A click&nbsp;
away was a section focused on life and family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:46.920
which professed Hines position that life begins&nbsp;
at conception. Naturally, the first thing greeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:53.760
any visitor to the site was the grinning face&nbsp;
of Trump. And it is all gone, gone, gone, gone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:00.840
And the article points out that all of that stuff,&nbsp;
all of those invocations and images of Donald&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:08.640
Trump, they've all been removed, except for one&nbsp;
apparently photoshopped image with Hines in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:15.420
section on border security. This is not the first&nbsp;
person who did this. The Daily Beast reviewed a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:19.740
bunch of different websites for 2022 Republican&nbsp;
candidates and found that at least five House&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:26.400
Republican candidates in battleground districts&nbsp;
completely wiped or scrubbed mentions of Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:32.340
or the 2020 election from their websites or social&nbsp;
media right after winning the primaries. At least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:37.500
seven removed or significantly modified language&nbsp;
about abortion on their websites over the summer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:43.620
At least three have scrubbed their online pages&nbsp;
of Trump. That includes Blake Masters of Arizona,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:50.040
Mehmet Oz of Pennsylvania, Tiffany Smiley of&nbsp;
Washington, and at least two did the same for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:58.500
abortion, Blake Masters and Adam Laxalt. So, as&nbsp;
the article says, generally it's uncommon to see.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:03.900
And that it's significantly changed platforms in&nbsp;
branding during the course of a campaign. Some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:08.640
of them are saying, well, we just redirect. We&nbsp;
just redesigned the website. That's all we did.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:14.940
Others are saying, well, we reshuffled the content&nbsp;
to other places, but we know exactly what is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:22.200
on here. There is a recognition among many of&nbsp;
these Republican candidates that, number one, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:29.640
hard line anti-abortion positions are out of step&nbsp;
with the American public. We know that all polling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:35.820
shows us that over the last 50 years, what we call&nbsp;
the Roe v Wade era, over the period of the Roe v&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:44.280
Wade era. The country has moved in the direction&nbsp;
of abortion being legal to those who want it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:50.700
most cases. And this really came to a head with&nbsp;
the Supreme Court's reversal of Roe v Wade with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:56.700
guaranteed abortion as a right, in most cases&nbsp;
to women. This is now causing a problem for some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:01.680
Republicans. And the way some of them are dealing&nbsp;
with it is getting rid of the hardcore anti-choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:07.800
language from their websites. But there's a second&nbsp;
bigger recognition as well. The guy that will help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:14.100
you win among the Republican electorate may&nbsp;
actually hurt you in the general election. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:18.960
we're talking, of course, of Donald Trump. This&nbsp;
is, to some degree a change from previous years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:24.300
You know, we often laugh about Trump takes credit&nbsp;
for his endorsement record. And that record really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:30.300
isn't quite as good as he claims it to be. But&nbsp;
the truth is that it was pretty good for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:39.540
and it no longer is seen as the sort of unabashed&nbsp;
advantage that it was at one point. We will see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:45.240
how these folks do in November, and then&nbsp;
we will see what that means for 2024.

